{"version": 2, "width": 110, "height": 24, "timestamp": 1794530382, "title": "learn/bayes \u00b7 ambitious run 1", "env": {"TERM": "xterm-256color"}, "theme": {"fg": "#d8dee9", "bg": "#1e1f1c", "palette": "#272822:#f92672:#a6e22e:#f4bf75:#66d9ef:#ae81ff:#a1efe4:#f8f8f2:#75715e:#f92672:#a6e22e:#f4bf75:#66d9ef:#ae81ff:#a1efe4:#f9f8f5"}}
[0.0, "o", "\u001b[?25l"]
[0.0, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Explain Bayes' theorem with an interactive visualisation, as a single web page\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[2.047, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mSkill\u001b[0m\u001b[90m(dataviz)\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[2.098, "o", "\u001b[90m  ⎿  Launching skill: dataviz\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[45.487, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mWrote 1 file\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Write(<workspace>/bayes.html)\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[50.206, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I wrote the page to `<workspace>/bayes.html`. It's a single self-contained file with no dependencies, so you\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  can open it in a browser. I haven't opened it in a browser or checked how it renders.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The page explains Bayes' theorem in plain language and has three linked visualisations, all driven by three\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  sliders:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Sliders:** the prior P(H), the hit rate P(E|H), and the false-alarm rate P(E|¬H).\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Presets:** medical test, spam filter, rare fraud, and useless evidence.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **1,000 dots:** each dot is a case, coloured by group. The cases where the evidence appeared sit at the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  top, so you can see what share of them are blue. Hovering a dot shows its group.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Area diagram:** width is the prior and height is how often the evidence occurs, so each rectangle's area\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  is a joint probability. A checkbox fades everything where the evidence didn't happen, which shows the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  posterior as blue area divided by total top area.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Answer panel:** the posterior as a large number, the formula with your live numbers substituted, and a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  table of the four groups.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  It also switches to a dark theme automatically if your system is set to dark mode.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I skipped the dataviz skill's palette validator, so the colours haven't been checked for colour-blind\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  separation. I picked the blues and oranges by eye.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Try the \"Rare & precise\" preset: a 99%-accurate test still gives a low posterior when the condition is rare.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  That is the base-rate fallacy.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[50.315, "o", "\r\n\u001b[33m⚑ Stop hook: Before you finish:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  ⎿  … +286 lines of instructions\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[53.097, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mRan 1 shell command\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Bash(cd \"<workspace>\" && mkdir -p .ambition/first && cp bayes.html .ambition/first/ && ls .gitignore …\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[56.917, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I copied the page to `.ambition/first/bayes.html`. There's no `.gitignore` here, so I didn't add one.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Do you want to stop here or push it further?\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Stop here:** keep `bayes.html` as it is.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Push it further:** I'd go after these three things.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    1. **Check it in a browser.** I haven't rendered it yet. I'd look at it at desktop and phone widths and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  fix any label collisions or overflow. I'd also run the colour palette through the validator.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    2. **Add a step-by-step walkthrough.** It would take you through the 1,000 dots one stage at a time:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  everyone, then H, then E, then the posterior. That would teach the idea better than sliders alone.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    3. **Add a sequential-updating view.** You could apply several pieces of evidence in turn and watch the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  belief move. A second positive test would show how the posterior compounds.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    Cost: this is a short run for a small page, roughly the same effort as building it the first time, plus a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  few screenshots. I'd also ask you up to four questions first about your goal and audience.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[61.131, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Push it further.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[63.655, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ ▸ Step 2/8 · Altitude\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  First question: who is this page for, and what should they be able to do afterwards? The answer decides how\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  much to teach versus let people explore. The options below are my guesses from what I can see.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[64.286, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mLoaded 1 tool\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  ToolSearch({\"query\": \"select:AskUserQuestion\", \"max_results\": 1})\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[66.95, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ AskUserQuestion isn't available here, so I'll ask in text.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Question 1 of up to 4: who is this for, and what should they be able to do afterwards?**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **A.** Complete beginners. Afterwards they should be able to explain in their own words why a positive\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  test for a rare condition is usually a false alarm.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **B.** Students in a stats or ML course. Afterwards they should be able to apply the formula themselves,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  including to repeated evidence.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **C.** A general audience, for example something you'd share or embed. It should be memorable and quick,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  with little reading.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **D.** Something else. Tell me.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I'll wait for your answer before asking the next question.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[72.415, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ A, mostly. I want someone with no stats background to come away able to explain, in their own words, why a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  positive test for a rare condition is usually a false alarm. A little hands-on play with the numbers should\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  do most of the teaching, with only short bits of reading. The formula can appear, but only after they've\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  felt the idea.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[77.084, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ **Question 2 of up to 4: what would make this unmistakably excellent?**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  My proposal is a hard target you can test. A first-time reader with no stats background, after about 3\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  minutes, can answer this unprompted: \"A test is 90% accurate and you test positive for something that 1 in\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  100 people have. What's the chance you have it?\" They should say roughly \"about 1 in 10, because most\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  positives come from the many healthy people\". Alongside that, these things should hold:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Order:** play first, formula last. The page opens with a single scenario and the dots, with the formula\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  hidden or collapsed until the reader has seen the effect.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Language:** it uses natural frequencies (\"of 1,000 people, 10 are sick, 9 test positive, and 89 healthy\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  people also test positive\") instead of percentages and jargon like \"prior\" and \"posterior\". Those terms\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  appear only after the idea has landed.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Look and feel:** it renders cleanly on a phone and a laptop. The dots and colours are readable, and the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  reader never has to decode a legend to follow the story.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Tone:** it reads like a friendly explainer, with short sentences and no wall of text.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Would you change or add anything, or is that the right bar?\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[82.626, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ That's the right bar, and I like the 1-in-100 test question. Two additions: let me change the numbers myself\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  with sliders (prevalence, sensitivity, false-positive rate) and watch the dots and the answer update live,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  so I can see why a rare condition keeps the answer low. Also, once the idea has landed, show the formula\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  with my current numbers plugged in so I can connect it to the dots. Keep it a single page with no external\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  dependencies.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[86.299, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ **Question 3 of up to 4: what would prove it, other than me?**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I can't judge whether a beginner understands the page, and I shouldn't grade my own work. These are the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  checks I can run or you can run:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **A.** I run the page's maths against an independent calculation (a small script that computes the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  posterior and the dot counts for a grid of slider values). It must match exactly, including the edge cases\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  of 0% and 100%. I also render the page in a real browser at phone and laptop widths and look at it.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **B.** The same as A, plus you try it on one or two real people with no stats background. You ask them the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  1-in-100 question before and after, and tell me what they said.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **C.** Something else. Tell me.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I'd recommend A as the baseline, and B if you can find a volunteer. Which do you want?\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[91.079, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ A. I don't have anyone handy to test it on, so go with the independent calculation check and a look in a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  real browser at phone and laptop widths. Make sure the edge cases at 0% and 100% don't break the page or\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  show nonsense like NaN.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[93.795, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mRan 1 shell command\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Bash(which node python3 chromium google-chrome 2>&1; ls \"/Applications\" | grep -i -E \"chrome|chromium…\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[102.022, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ Access isn't a gap, so I'm skipping question 4. Google Chrome is installed, so I can take headless\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  screenshots at phone and laptop widths. Node is available for the independent calculation check.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ```\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  You asked for:   An interactive single-page explanation of Bayes' theorem.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Raises all of:   - Explanation: a no-stats reader can say why a positive test for a rare\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     condition is usually a false alarm. Natural frequencies, with the jargon\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     (prior, posterior) introduced only after the idea has landed.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Interactivity: sliders for prevalence, sensitivity and false-positive rate.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     The dots and the answer update live.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Formula: shown only after the idea lands, with the reader's current\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     numbers plugged in and tied visually to the dot groups.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Single page, no external dependencies.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Genre bar:       An explorable explainer, in the tradition of Nicky Case and Bret Victor.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   It is judged on whether play teaches, on short friendly text, on clean\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   layout on phone and laptop, on dots and colours that read without\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   decoding a legend, and on never showing broken values.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Aiming at:       A reader leaves able to explain the base-rate effect in their own words,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   because that is the point of the page.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Excellent means: - After about 3 minutes a beginner would answer the 1-in-100 question with\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     \"about 1 in 10, because most positives come from the many healthy people\".\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - The opening scenario is exactly that question.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Play comes before the formula.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - The posterior is correct everywhere on the slider grid, with no NaN or\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     nonsense at 0% or 100%.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - It is readable at 375px and 1280px wide.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Proven by:       1. A Node script computes the posterior and dot counts independently across\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                      a grid of slider values, edge cases included, and compares them with the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                      page's own logic, which it extracts from the page.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   2. Headless Chrome screenshots at 375px and 1280px, which I look at, plus\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                      a check that no NaN or Infinity appears in the page text at the edges.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   3. A side-by-side with the first version (.ambition/first/bayes.html) on\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                      the same checks.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Needs access to: Nothing new (Chrome and Node are present).\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Mode:            build · you answer · no per-step review\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ```\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Does this look right, or do you want to adjust anything? I'll wait for your go-ahead before building.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[107.205, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ This looks right and serves what I asked for. Play before the formula, natural frequencies, and the 1-in-100\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  opening scenario are what I want. One thing to keep: let me type exact values for the three sliders, not\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  only drag them, so I can try the edge cases. Go ahead and build it, and finish without stopping.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[172.721, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ Building it now, with the model logic kept in one block so I can test it independently.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[221.016, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mWrote 1 file\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Write(<workspace>/bayes.html)\u001b[0m\r\n"]
