[534.12, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ **Step 4 report: Challenge**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Did:** I listed six assumptions and tested them in order of risk against the real page and real evidence.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I ran the real page with published-style screening numbers, which I hadn't used before. I also ran a web\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  search for the doctors claim.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Found:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  1. **The 1,000-dot assumption failed on real rare cases (the new blind spot).**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m     - For HIV-style screening (1 in 10,000 prevalence, 99.9% sensitivity, 0.01% false positives), the page\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  said \"nobody tests positive, so there's no answer\". The exact answer is 50%.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m     - For a newborn-screening case it showed \"6.22% (0 out of 1)\", and for another rare case \"3.48% (0 out of\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  10)\". Each was paired with \"none of them are really sick\", which contradicted the number.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m     - Cause: rounding to whole dots loses a rare group.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  2. **\"Most people say 90%, including many doctors\" was overclaimed.** The evidence supports that physicians\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  badly overestimate: Eddy (1982) found guesses of about 70–80% against a true figure of about 8%\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ([source](https://pure.mpg.de/pubman/item/item_2101266_7/component/file_2563079/az0400101.pdf)). Hoffrage &\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Gigerenzer found only 10% of 48 physicians correct with probabilities, and 46% with natural frequencies\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ([PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9609869/)). The evidence does not show \"most people say 90%\".\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  3. **\"It's true whenever the condition is rare\" was too broad.** A rare condition with a very low false-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  positive rate gives a high answer, as the \"never cries wolf\" preset shows.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Changed:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Zoom-out for rare cases:** when 1,000 dots can't show the rare group, the story, answer, bar and formula\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  switch to a bigger crowd (10,000, 100,000 and so on) whose whole-person counts match the exact answer. The\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  text says what it's doing. The bar and the \"mostly false alarms\" verdict now come from the exact numbers.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Prevalence below 1 in 1,000:** step 2 says no dot is blue at this size.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Wording:** the intro now says \"whenever a condition is rare and the test makes even a few mistakes\". The\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  90% feedback now cites the classic studies instead of \"most people\".\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Checks:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Independent calculation:** 4,096 combinations plus 5 real-world cases pass, with 0 failures.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Interaction test:** 24 of 24 pass.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Looked at:** I looked at the rare cases on a phone-width frame.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Limits:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Other browsers:** I only tested Chrome. Safari and Firefox are untested. The page uses `color-mix()` and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  CSS transforms on SVG, which recent Safari and Firefox support. I'm relying on that, not on a test.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Extremely rare cases:** at around 1 in 10 million, the 1,000-dot view still shows \"0 of 5\" and says none\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  are sick, which is only roughly right. It stays within a 1.5-point tolerance of the exact answer (0.002%).\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The step copy is in `.ambition/steps/4/`.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[534.209, "o", "\r\n\u001b[33m⚑ Stop hook: Step 5 of pushing the work further: Range. Open your first message with this line on its own,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m  exactly: `▸ Step 5/8 · Range`. Write that line nowhere else.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  ⎿  … +15 lines of instructions\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[575.099, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ ▸ Step 5/8 · Range\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What this is really trying to do, as verbs.** Let a stranger *predict* a counterintuitive fact, *feel* it\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  by *manipulating* the numbers, *explain it back* in their own words, and *connect* it to a formula, without\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  *misleading* anyone about a real test result. I'm keeping the page's own verbs: *explain*, *let them play*,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  *teach*.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Fields, the method I'm borrowing from each, and the direction taken**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Field | What they actually do | Direction taken |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  |---|---|---|\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | **Explorable-explanation design** (the genre's best practitioner: Nicky Case, Bret Victor) | Make the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  reader commit to a prediction, then give them a goal to pursue by manipulating the model, not just free\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  sliders | A **challenge card**: \"Make a positive result more likely real than a false alarm\", with live\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  feedback as sliders move |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | **Learning science** (the field I wouldn't normally reach for) | *Retrieval practice and self-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  explanation*: asking the learner to finish the explanation themselves beats re-reading | An **\"explain it\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  back\" card**: finish the sentence \"…because\" from three options, with feedback that corrects each wrong one\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | **Newsroom data journalism** | Annotate the chart directly, so the reader never decodes a legend to find\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the story | A label on the dot grid itself: \"↑ these 108 tested positive; you're one of them\" |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | **Clinical risk communication** (Cochrane decision aids) | Always state the reference class (\"of everyone\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  who tests positive…\") and never let a teaching number be read as personal advice | Answer wording names the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  reference class. A visible safety note. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | **Red team: patient-safety reviewer and adversarial user** | Ask who gets hurt if this is misread, and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  feed it hostile input | See below |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **The edge I took:** beginners would choose this over other explainers because it makes them *do* things\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  (guess, play, win a challenge, say it back) instead of reading, and it is honest about being a toy.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I'm applying these four now, then running the red team. First the page edits:\u001b[0m\r\n"]
