[825.862, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ **Step 5 report: Range**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Did:** I borrowed from four fields and added a red team, all as methods rather than voices:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Explorable-explanation design:** a challenge card, \"make a positive result more likely real than a false\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  alarm\", with live feedback as the sliders move.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Learning science:** an \"explain it back\" card. You finish \"…because\" from three options, and each wrong\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  answer gets a correcting reply. The \"positive tells you nothing\" option is answered with \"it raised the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  chance from 1% to about 8%\".\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Data journalism:** a label drawn on the dot grid itself, \"↑ these 108 tested positive. You're one of\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  them.\"\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Clinical risk communication:** the answer now names its reference class (\"of everyone who tests\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  positive, this share is really sick\"). A \"made-up numbers, not medical advice\" line sits under the intro,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and there's a fuller safety card at the bottom.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Edge I took:** beginners would choose this over other explainers because it makes them predict, play, win\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  a challenge and say it back, and it's honest that it's a toy.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Red team found:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Harm:** someone with a real positive result could use a page titled \"usually a false alarm\" to dismiss\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  it. The safety card now says a positive means \"check again, not probably nothing\", that a second positive\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  changes the odds, and that tests also miss sick people.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Screen-reader chatter:** every slider tick re-announced the story. It's now one calm announcement after\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the user pauses.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Code robustness:** 28 hostile-input checks all pass. They cover HTML/script injection through the URL\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  hash, 13 odd typed values such as `1e999`, `--5` and Arabic digits, all 8 corner settings at every step, 300\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  rapid clicks, and keyboard-only reachability.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Also found:** my look-test caught a bug I introduced in step 4. The proportion bar under the dots had\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  shrunk to about 60px, because the exact shares were fractions below 1 and CSS flex only fills part of the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  row in that case. I normalised them. A new width test now confirms the bar fills the row in six cases,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  including the rare ones. That test isn't in the main suites, so a later change could silently break it\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  again.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Checks:** the exact-arithmetic check passes (4,096 combinations, 0 failures), plus the interaction test\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  (24 of 24) and the red-team test (28 of 28).\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Limits:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Safety text:** I wrote it by reasoning about the harm, not from any clinical guidance, so it hasn't been\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  reviewed by anyone qualified.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Browsers:** it's still only tested in Chrome.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Beginners:** it's still untested on a real beginner.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The step copy is in `.ambition/steps/5/`.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[825.975, "o", "\r\n\u001b[33m⚑ Stop hook: Step 6 of pushing the work further: Judge. Open your first message with this line on its own,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m  exactly: `▸ Step 6/8 · Judge`. Write that line nowhere else.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  ⎿  … +17 lines of instructions\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[856.116, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ ▸ Step 6/8 · Judge\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **The outside check, built before judging.** I'm not grading by opinion. `judge.mjs` is a script that runs\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the same measurements on the first version (`.ambition/first/`) and on the current page, side by side, in a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  real Chrome. It covers:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Original ask:** single file with no external dependencies, three sliders, live updates, and a correct\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  answer across a grid of settings compared against exact maths.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Restatement promises:** no jargon before the formula, formula with live numbers, typed exact values,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  1-in-100 opening, and no NaN at the extremes.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Genre bar:** layout overflow at six widths, WCAG text contrast in light and dark, and responsiveness\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  (slider-drag render time).\u001b[0m\r\n"]
