[1176.391, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ ▸ Step 7/8 · Stretch\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **The target.** Your bar was that a beginner understands in about 3 minutes, with the page as 4 steps they\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  click through. I can't measure understanding without real people, so I'm stretching the part I can measure,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the **time and effort to reach the insight**:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Stretch:** from page load, **one click** and **≤ 12 seconds** to the full answer (the dots, the 108\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  positives, the 8.33%), with no reading homework in between. That's about 15× faster than 3 minutes and 3\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  clicks down to 1. It's a \"Play it for me\" mode, like the self-running explainers on Seeing Theory and Nicky\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Case's pages.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Fallback (yours):** the existing step-by-step path stays exactly as it is, and nothing about it changes.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Where it would likely break:** auto-advance text can go too fast to read, and a timer that can't be\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  stopped fails accessibility rules. So it needs a Pause button, and any manual action must cancel it. Also,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  12 seconds shows the answer; it doesn't prove understanding. The guess, challenge and explain-back are still\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  what do the teaching.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[1181.15, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mRan 1 shell command\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Bash(cd \"<workspace>\" && cp bayes.html .ambition/work/before_stretch.html && python3 -I - <<'EOF')\u001b[0m\r\n"]
